        <article id="fiji-iguana">
            <header>
                <h2>Animal Dossier: Fiji Island Banded Iguana</h2>
            </header>

            <section>
                <h3>Basic Information</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Common Name:</strong> Fiji Island Banded Iguana</li>
                    <li><strong>Status/Conservation:</strong> Critically Endangered 
                        </li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Full Scientific Classification</h3>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Kingdom</dt><dd>Animalia   </dd>
                    <dt>Phylum</dt><dd> Chordata </dd>
                    <dt>Subphylum</dt><dd>Vertebrata   </dd>
                    <dt>Class</dt><dd>Reptilia</dd>
                    <dt>Subclass</dt><dd>Diapsida </dd>
                    <dt>Order</dt><dd>Squamata</dd>
                    <dt>Suborder</dt><dd>Iguania </dd>
                    <dt>Family</dt><dd>Iguanidae</dd>
                    <dt>Genus</dt><dd>Brachylophus </dd>
                    <dt>Species</dt><dd>Brachylophus fasciatus </dd>
                </dl>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Physical Profile</h3>
                <p><strong>Size Range:</strong> 7.5 inches (19 cm)</p>
                <p><strong>Identification Markings:</strong> Fiji banded iguanas are bright green lizards with crested spines and long tails, which help them balance while climbing trees. Males have wide blue or light green stripes along their body. Females, on the other hand, are generally solid green but may have a few white or pale blue spots. These lizards have reddish-orange eyes and yellow nostrils. 
                </p>
                <figure>
                    <img src="https://science.sandiegozoo.org/sites/default/files/heros-fiji-iguana.jpg" alt="Fiji Island Banded Iguana physical profile alignment">
                    <figcaption>High-quality photograph of the Fiji Island Banded Iguana.</figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Habitat & Geography</h3>
                <p><strong>Habitat(s):</strong> These lizards tend to prefer the plants found in wet forests. They are arboreal, spending most of their time in trees. Therefore, they are found in areas with high vegetation and trees at least 20 feet (6 meters) tall. Like other iguanas, they are also strong swimmers.
                </p>
                <p><strong>Distribution:</strong> Fiji banded iguanas are only found on the Fijian islands. They are most prevalent on the central and northwestern islands, generally between 656 and 1,640 feet (200 and 500 meters) above sea level. They live in the mesic to moist forests of Viti Levu, Vanua Levu, Ovalau, Viwa, Kadavu and their associated islands.
                </p>
                <figure>
                    <img src="https://photographylife.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fijian-rainforest-habitat-of-fijian-banded-iguana.jpg" alt="Natural habitat of the Fiji Island Banded Iguana">
                    <figcaption>Natural habitat environment.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <figure>
                    <img src="https://s3.animalia.bio/animals/maps/medium/brachylophus-bulabula-distribution.webp?id=acf3132889cd4a78b9462bc1622c60ea" alt="Geographical distribution map of the Fiji Island Banded Iguana">
                    <figcaption>Distribution Map.</figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Ecology & Behavior</h3>
                <h4>Feeding and Diet</h4>
                <p>They are primarily herbivorous and forage for leaves, flowers and fruit. On a rare occasion, they may also eat insects. </p>
                
                <h4>Behaviors and Adaptations</h4>
                <p>Most of the iguana's day is spent in the trees basking in the sunlight. The Fiji banded iguana is diurnal, spending part of each day foraging, basking, and watching over their territory.</p>
                
                <h4>Communication</h4>
                <p>When the highly visual male comes across another male, he will aggressively defend his territory by bobbing its head in a warning. The more excited he is, the faster his head moves. He will deepen his green coloration to intensify his bands. He will attack with an open mouth and possibly expand the dewlap to appear larger. 
                </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Life History Cycle & Breeding Behaviors</h3>
                <p>Fiji banded iguanas reach reproductive maturity at 3 to 4 years old. Males court females by bobbing their heads and flicking their tongues. After copulation, females dig burrows in nesting sites to lay their eggs. They generally lay a clutch of about five eggs, which incubate for seven to nine months.  </p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Predators</h3>
                <p> Predators are the black rat, Indian mongoose, and feral cats</p>
            </section>
        </article>

        <article id="blue-throated-macaw">
            <header>
                <h2>Animal Dossier: Blue-throated Macaw</h2>
            </header>

            <section>
                <h3>Basic Information</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Common Name:</strong> Blue-throated Macaw</li>
                    <li><strong>Status/Conservation:</strong> Critically Endangered</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Full Scientific Classification</h3>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Kingdom</dt><dd> Animalia</dd>
                    <dt>Phylum</dt><dd>Chordata </dd>
                    <dt>Subphylum</dt><dd>Vertebrata</dd>
                    <dt>Class</dt><dd> Aves</dd>
                    <dt>Subclass</dt><dd>Neornithes </dd>
                    <dt>Order</dt><dd> Psittaciformes</dd>
                    <dt>Suborder</dt><dd> Psittaci </dd>
                    <dt>Family</dt><dd>Psittacidae</dd>
                    <dt>Genus</dt><dd>Ara</dd>
                    <dt>Species</dt><dd>A. glaucogularis</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Physical Profile</h3>
                <p><strong>Size Range:</strong>2'6" - 2'9"</p>
                <p><strong>Identification Markings:</strong>  Blue-throated macaws have vibrant turquoise-blue feathers covering their throat, head, back, and the upper parts of their wings and tail. A stripe of golden-yellow feathers is seen between the top of their head and on the sides of their face. This golden-yellow coloration goes to the underside of their body, wings, and tail. Their face has a sparsely feathered skin patch near the base of their dark beak, with five or six horizontal blue feather stripes unique to each bird, which can help identify adults. Newly hatched macaws are pink and featherless, developing gray down before their colored feathers emerge.  
                    </p>
                <figure>
                    <img src="https://abcbirds.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/1500x2500_Blue-throated-Macaw-Daniel-Alarcon-Asociacion-Armonia-American-Bird-Conservancy-1229x2048.webp" alt="Blue-throated Macaw physical profile alignment">
                    <figcaption>High-quality photograph of the Blue-throated Macaw.</figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Habitat & Geography</h3>
                <p><strong>Habitat(s):</strong> Blue-throated macaws live, eat, and nest in trees. Their main way of getting around is flying, but they can also climb trees, move along branches, and walk on the ground. These birds are active during the day and usually stay in one general area. However, occasional sightings in Paraguay suggest they sometimes travel long distances. 
                </p>
                <p><strong>Distribution:</strong> native to the palm forests of Bolivia</p>
                <figure>
                    <img src="https://abcbirds.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Laney-Rickman-Reserve-Bolivia-by-Tjalle-BoorsmaAsociacion-Armonia-1536x864.webp" alt="Natural habitat of the Blue-throated Macaw">
                    <figcaption>Natural habitat environment.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <figure>
                    <img src="https://s3.animalia.bio/animals/maps/medium/ze87datwb37kzoZybIZz.webp?id=c8351e557f647fe77d15ff7c68abf40a" alt="Geographical distribution map of the Blue-throated Macaw">
                    <figcaption>Distribution Map.</figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Ecology & Behavior</h3>
                <h4>Feeding and Diet</h4>
                <p>Blue-throated macaws don't eat as many seeds and nuts as other macaws do. They mostly eat fruit from big palm trees. These macaws eat the fleshy part of ripe fruit. Sometimes, they have also been seen drinking the juices from very young fruit.  
                </p>
                
                <h4>Behaviors and Adaptations</h4>
                <p>Blue-throated macaws are social birds as they form monogamous mating pairs and will also live in small groups. One group of 70 individuals was known at one point, but this species is not often seen in large flocks as other species of macaws have. This is most likely because blue-throated macaws are very rare and have such a low population that the formation of such large groups is very unlikely. </p>
                
                <h4>Communication</h4>
                <p>[Insert Communication text]</p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Life History Cycle & Breeding Behaviors</h3>
                <p> A mating pair of blue-throated macaws can be very affectionate towards one another and are often seen preening each other's feathers and perching close together.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
                <h3>Predators</h3>
                <p>Adults can be preyed upon by great horned owls , southern caracaras, coati, tayras, and brown capuchin monkeys. Nestlings are preyed upon by crane hawks, while both eggs and nestlings are eaten by toco toucans. 

                </p>
            </section>
        </article>

